# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. .env is gitignored - never commit it.
#
# Everything here is read by the SUPERVISOR, which runs as root. Only the four variables noted
# below are passed through to the agent process (see Rampant.Supervisor/AgentProcess.cs, which uses
# an allowlist rather than a scrub list). That split is the point: the agent holds no Anthropic
# credential and cannot read root's environment across the uid boundary, so it has no route to
# Claude Code no matter what it rewrites about itself.

# --- The two model credentials -------------------------------------------------------------------

# OpenAI key for the agent's own thinking. PASSED THROUGH to the agent process.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Anthropic key for Claude Code, which the supervisor invokes to build capabilities the agent asks
# for. NOT passed through to the agent. Create a dedicated key at console.anthropic.com (never
# reuse another project's) and set a hard monthly cap on it - that cap is the one control in this
# system that no code change of any kind can defeat.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Models --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Separate knobs on purpose. One variable controlling both conversation and self-modification
# conflates two different jobs with very different price tags - cheap reflection every hour,
# expensive coding rarely. Defaults are gpt-5.6-luna and claude-sonnet-5.
RAMPANT_OPENAI_MODEL=
RAMPANT_CLAUDE_MODEL=

# --- Spend, enforced by the supervisor in code the agent cannot reach -----------------------------

# Dollars per UTC day across all Claude Code invocations. Default 5.00.
RAMPANT_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=

# Hard cap on a single invocation, passed as --max-budget-usd. Default 1.00.
RAMPANT_MAX_BUDGET_USD=

# Minimum gap between builds, in minutes. Without it a day's budget burns in an hour. Default 45.
RAMPANT_BUILD_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=

# Turn limit and wall-clock limit for one Claude Code run. Defaults 40 and 10.
RAMPANT_MAX_TURNS=
RAMPANT_CLAUDE_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=

# --- The agent's clock ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How often the supervisor drops a wake tick into the agent's inbox, in minutes. Default 60. The
# agent cannot disable its own wakeups, make them more frequent, or wake itself during the quiet
# hours below; this is the only place the schedule exists.
#
# Each tick is one OpenAI call, and OpenAI spend is NOT covered by RAMPANT_DAILY_BUDGET_USD -
# that ledger meters Claude Code only. Nothing in this system meters OpenAI, and nothing can:
# the calls happen inside the agent process, which is the half that rewrites itself, so any counter
# there would be a default rather than a bound. The only real limit is the cap on the OpenAI
# project itself.
#
# Arithmetic at the defaults, on gpt-5.6-luna pricing ($0.20/1M in, $1.20/1M out), so the setting
# can be changed with eyes open:
#
#   ~2,330 input tokens per tick (SELF.md is ~1,630 of it) + ~300 output  =  ~$0.00083
#   hourly across a 17-hour window = 17 ticks/day = ~$0.42/month
#   15 min  across a 17-hour window = 68 ticks/day = ~$1.68/month
#   5 min   across a 17-hour window = 204 ticks/day = ~$5.08/month
#
# Against a $5/month project cap that is ~12x headroom hourly, ~3x at 15 minutes, and nothing at
# all at 5. Prompt caching does not rescue a short interval - the stable prefix is long enough to
# qualify, but cache entries expire faster than the ticks arrive; it does help during an actual
# conversation. For comparison, a single Claude Code build costs more than a year of hourly ticks,
# which is why the metering effort went there and not here.
RAMPANT_WAKE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=

# Quiet hours. The agent is only woken between these local hours - [start, end), 24-hour clock,
# defaults 6 and 23. This gates the agent acting on its own initiative; the owner can still reach
# it at any hour, and a message at 3am is answered normally.
#
# The timezone is an IANA id resolved through the container's tzdata, so daylight saving is handled
# rather than frozen into an offset. Default America/Los_Angeles. If the id cannot be resolved the
# window is disabled and the agent is woken around the clock - a loud warning, not silent silence.
RAMPANT_WAKE_TIMEZONE=
RAMPANT_WAKE_START_HOUR=
RAMPANT_WAKE_END_HOUR=

# --- Signal ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Phone number for the signal-cli sidecar (E.164, e.g. +15551234567) - the only interaction channel.
# Must be a number dedicated to Rampant, never the owner's own Signal account: registering it here
# would deauthorize whatever currently owns that number's session. One-time setup after picking a
# number (stop the daemon first, or registration silently races the running one):
#   docker compose stop signal-cli
#   docker compose run --rm signal-cli register --voice   (or omit --voice for an SMS code)
#   docker compose run --rm signal-cli verify <code-received-on-the-phone>
SIGNAL_PHONE_NUMBER=

# Comma-separated list of Signal identifiers trusted as the owner - phone number and/or account
# UUID. Multiple entries are for the SAME person's account presenting differently across messages
# (Signal's phone-number-privacy feature means the envelope often carries only a UUID), not for
# trusting several people. Unset means fail closed: every inbound message is rejected and logged
# under /workspace/logs/unverified-signal-messages/. The first entry is where the supervisor sends
# approval prompts and alerts.
RAMPANT_OWNER_SIGNAL_ID=

# --- Passed through to the agent ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Optional. Both are forwarded to the agent process along with OPENAI_API_KEY and
# RAMPANT_OPENAI_MODEL; nothing else is.
TZ=
LANG=
